  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 AM Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A typical late summer weather pattern continues \ntoday, with highs in the 90s and isolated showers and thunderstorms \npossible. \n\nWith little airmass change over central NC, a very similar weather \npattern to previous days can be expected. Surface high pressure \nlocated off the coast continues to bring warm, moist air into the \nregion from the south, with quasi-zonal flow aloft. This will allow \nfor maximum temperatures expected in the low/mid 90s once again, \nwith maximum heat indices in the mid 90s to around 100. \n\nThis afternoon and evening, a shortwave trough looks to move across \nthe region, providing an overall weak source of lift for isolated \nafternoon and evening showers and embedded thunderstorms. Limited \nshear values (< 20 kts) will limit severe potential, however there \nwill be plenty of instability, with MUCAPE values expected to reach \naround 2000-2500 J/kg this afternoon. Thus, a few strong to severe \nwind gusts from water-loaded downdrafts cannot be ruled out. \nAdditionally, widespread flooding is not expected, however showers \nshould be efficient rain producers so isolated instances of flash \nflooding cannot be ruled out, especially if storms backbuild or \ntrain over one area. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Summertime heat and humidity will continue this \nweek, though probably lessened/interrupted by a series of MCSs and \nrelated outflow and debris cloudiness - and a couple of which which \nmay influence cntl NC with accompanying strong-severe wind potential \nby Tue and Wed. \n\nAround a sub-tropical ridge that will retrograde from the swrn N. \nAtlantic to the Deep South/TN Valley, cntl NC will be influenced by \na series of (mostly convectively-enhanced) mid-level disturbances in \nwnwly flow aloft from the upr MS Valley to the srn Middle Atlantic.\n\nAt the surface, an Appalachian lee trough will extend across the \nCarolinas this week, between high pressure across the sub-tropical \nAtlantic and Gulf and a convectively-modulated frontal zone that \nwill waver from the central Plains to the southern middle Atlantic. \nThe models suggest the synoptic frontal zone may edge into the \nCarolinas by Fri night-Sat.\n\nWhile the prevailing wnwly flow pattern aloft will be favorable for \nMCS development around the sub-tropical ridge, mesoscale \nuncertainties with their timing and track will have implications on \nboth precipitation and wind potential into cntl NC and also impacts \nto what will otherwise be a hot and seasonably humid environment \nover cntl NC. Despite those uncertainties, the CAMs to varying \ndegrees depict at least one vigorous MCS moving across the Virginias \nand into at least nrn NC on Tue. Its related outflow and debris \ncloudiness would then have ramifications on the track and \ndevelopment of subsequent convective clusters into the Carolinas on \nWed. Machine learning guidance indicates potential Slight risk \nprobabilities for severe wind both Tue and Wed in cntl NC. \n\nIt will otherwise be hot for most of the week, between bouts of \nconvection/outflow/cloudiness, with temperatures well into the 90s \nand with heat index values in the upr 90s to near 100 over the nw \nPiedmont to around 105 elsewhere, through at least Thu. The approach \nand possible passage of the aforementioned frontal zone will \nprobably yield less hot temperatures by Sat.",
